{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target asserts a 'shares mechanism' relation with SRC_008 but provides no causal mechanism linking the two artifacts: it labels the slot-replacement model a 'shared structural principle' yet never explains how a positional-swap rule is inherited, instantiated, or operationalized across the two endpoints. The 'Refuted if' clause merely restates boundary conditions on the slot model, not a mechanism connecting SRC_001 to SRC_008. Under a demand-for-causal-mechanism standard, naming a formal pattern and asserting it is shared is a summary of content, not a substantive relation.","target":"05bc0deeb0a55ae2a286bdd351146c4e4e951d33cbf5dce14d3b2e0095912afe"},{"attack":false,"case":"","target":"769c592a4612500ca1726d1f5a2330ca40d14874699d23a4de650f3b50af7ef7"},{"attack":true,"case":"The target claims to 'abstract' a general principle from SRC_005 and SRC_008, but it identifies no causal mechanism by which the abstraction is derived or validated—it simply posits an ordinal-slot-transfer rule and asserts it applies. A causal-mechanism stance requires the abstraction relation to show how the shared mechanism is extracted from the source artifacts and grounded in a common process; instead, the target restates a formal rule and applies it, which is a summary-plus-application, not a demonstrated abstraction relation.","target":"b99a2d5b0214d72701d8dd056b535174bf5211411f31b1e48c57d3f1d3f79b7d"}]}